Egypt returned about 30 thousand merchandise-slapped from the country of artifacts
Over the last 10 years, the government of Cairo managed to return to his homeland about 30 thousand artifacts, which were smuggled from the country by smuggled.
This was announced on Saturday, December 11, on the Al-Hayat TV channel the chief inspector of returned antiquities of the Ministry of tourism and antiquities of Egypt Shaaban Abdel Gauad.
Since in 2011, the Egyptian state managed to recover 29,300 artifacts.
Only this year, 5,300 Monuments of Ancient Egyptian Civilization have been discovered at the Biblical Museum in D.C.
Abdel Gauad clarified that all artifacts smuggled are registered in the archive.
Now employees of the Department are working on their return to Egypt.
In particular, experts "track auctions around the world and sales on the Internet" through foreign and Egyptian sites, as well as social networks.
The chief inspector said that on the return of cultural values will be restored, and then exhibited in museums.
According to him, one of the returned artifacts was discovered in Belgium an ancient skeleton of a man, which is now part of an exhibition of the National Museum of the Egyptian Civilization in Circumstances.
In 2020, the Egyptian authorities appealed to Interpol with a request to help to find a bust sold at an auquar in London by pharaoh Tutankhamun.
Auction house Christie's on July 4 held auctions, on which the bust of the age of more than 3 thousand years was sold for $ 5.97 million (about 680 million rubles).
This caused dissatisfaction from Egypt.
the Egyptian side also claims that the object of art was smuggled in the 1970-ies from the Karnak temple in Luksor.
Tutankhamun's head, depicted in the image of the sun god Amun, was stored in a private collection and has not been put up for auction since 1985.
How the relic came to Europe is unknown.
In recent years, Egypt has been actively seeking the repatriation of smuggled monuments of antiquity from the country at different times.
According to the authorities, 95% of smuggled antiquities are the result of archaeological research of “black copiers”.
