FT: Credit Suisse wants to get access to personal mobile devices of Employees
Credit Suisse requires its Employees to grant the bank access to their personal mobile devices, writes the Financial Times.
The fact is that in Europe and the U.S. the Swiss bank does not give its employees corporate communication devices, instead proposing a discount on the purchase of a mobile phone or a smartphone from a partner - a telecommunications operator.
According to the current rules, the bank can view information on corporate devices issued to its employees, as those are used to solve business issues with colleagues or customers.
The bank enjoys the right to inspect mobile devices in the most extreme cases - for example, if the employee used it to meet a client that has not been approved by Credit Suisse.
Now now, after many employees have switched to a remote mode of operation, the bank wants to extend this rule to all the devices that have been used to solve working issues.
According to the newspaper, the management of the bank has set a deadline to which employees can familiarize themselves with a new rule.
Officially in Credit Suisse information about a new requirement are not commented.
