Disclosed ways to return the money stallened through online stores
There are a number of ways to recover money stolen through online stores, and not become a victim of scam in the future.
They were disclosed by experts on information security in an interview with RBC.
So, in order to reduce the risk of large losses when these bank cards fall, it is advisable to store fixed assets on a card or bank account, which are not used for online shopping.
Part of the savings can be postponed to a savings account and displayed as needed.
The experts also recommend to enter a separate shopping card on the network.
If the money was stewed when shopping in stores that don’t use 3D Secure protective technology, you can launch a refund procedure.
“As part of the rules established by the payment systems under unauthorised transactions carried out without the use of 3D Secure, responsibility rests with the participant in the settlement chain who does not use this service”, – said the representative of Promsvyazbank.
The expert from the international payment provider Ecommpay Artur Zaremba stressed that the buyer has the right to apply to the bank to challenge the fraudulent operation no later than 120 days after the purchase.
In addition, if the offender used the card on online resources that do not require two-factor authorization, the transaction can be challenged and returned money.
It is noted that in some cases, online stores themselves return the funds to the client, if the order has not yet been sent for delivery.
On November 19, a member of the Public Chamber, the Director of the Sabotage Internet League Ekaterina Mizulina said that only in 2021, 11 thousand appeals were received on various cases of fraud: from the sale of forged documents to the the theft of personal data.
According to her, an effective decision under the current circumstances can be the creation of a single window for complaints about online scammers.
Scholz arrived in Poland to discuss “Northern stream-2” and EU legislation
The newly-elect German Chancellor Olaf Scholz arrived in Varshav for negotiations dedicated to the crisis with migrants on the border with Belarus, the independence of the judicial power in Poland, the expansion of Russian troops near the borders of Ukraine and the situation with the Russian gas pipeline “Northern stream-2”.
Although Scholz stated his guise of maintaining good relations between Germany and Poland for many years, the leaders of the two countries expressed unequivourable expectations from each other.
The Prime Minister of Poland Mateusz Moravetsky said that he will urge Scholz to speak out “Northern stream-2”, according to which it is planned to deliver Russian gas to Germany by bypassing Ukraine.
According to Moravetsky, Russia can use the gas pipeline against Europe as a means of political pressure.
In turn, Germany supported the efforts of Poland to stop the flow of migrants who are trying to enter the country from Belarus.
Berlin said it would help Warsaw and Brusels to resolve the crisis.
The judicial system of Poland, however, remains one of the reasons for disagreement.
The European court fined Poland, steetting that the judicial reforms carried out by the right-wing “Law and Equity”, violated EU legislation.
Poland refused to pay a fine, and its highest court ruled that national legislation took precedence over EU rules.
The new German government, sworn in on Wednesday, despite the demands of Poland and the United States, did not undertake a public obligation to stop the work of the “Nord stream-2” in the event of Russia’s invasion of Ukraine.
U.S. officials told members of Congress that they had reached an understanding with Germany about stopping the gas pipeline if Russia invaded Ukraine, a senior U.S. official said Tuesday.
German officials did not confirm these reports, but Scholz on Wednesday warned about the consequences if Russia breaks the border of Ukraine, and this position on Sunday was confirmed by representatives of the “Big seven”.
