New Caledonia Refuses Independence from France
According to the final results of the Third Referendum on New Caledonia's independence, the vast majority of participants refused to secede from France.
This was reported on December 12 by the French TV channel BFM.TV.
Against the independence spoke 96.5% of voters.
3.51% of residents of the archipelago in the Southern African Ocean were independent of France.
Annuled the results of 2.99% of the bulletins.
The level of participation in the vote on this day was very low: about 55% of the inhabitants of the archipelago came to the polling stations.
According to experts, the low turnout is explained by the calls of the separatists to refuse to participate in the referendum due to the coronavirus.
Paris refused to reschedule the date of the referendum.
In October 2020 the residents of New Caledonia spoke out in a referendum against independence from France.
In 2018, there has already been a referendum on the issue of independence in the region.
The majority of residents supported the presence of an archipelago as part of France.
New Caledonia is located almost 20 thousand kilometers from mainland France.
In 1853 France declared New Caledonia its possession.
About 270 thousand people live in the archipelago, the territory is endowed with a special administrative-territorial status.
Between Paris and the administration of New Caledonia, there is an agreement of 1998, according to which the region can organize a vote on its independence twice with a difference of two years.
