Estonian scientists will look for life on Venus
The Massachusetts Institute of Technology, in cooperation with the Tartu observatory and other research institutions and companies, prepares three space missions for Venus to look for signs of life in the atmosphere, “Actual Camera” reported on Sunday.
The purpose of missions starting in the five-eat five-eater is to evaluate the suitability for the living of the clouds of Venus, and then the search for life there.
For the project, Tartu scientists proposed tools to measure the acidity and the oxygen content in the clouds of Venus.
Until now it was assumed that the clouds of Venus, closest to the Ethnic planet, are too acidic to have life to exist there, but it is probably originally Venus being an terrestrial planet.
During the third mission, a sample from Venus must arrive.
Let us assume that if we find life there, the main question will be whether it looks like an terrestrial terrestrial one.
If it is very similar, it will be obvious that terrestrial life did not begin on Earth.
If it is different, it may become more likely that life originated on Earth, ”said Mihkel Payusalu, the head of the Estonian team of Venus Life Finder.
